================================================================================
2. DATA — all rights reserved
================================================================================

Applies to the data files in this repository and to the data served from it:
*.csv, jobs_data.json, history.csv, companies.json, and any file whose content
is a collection of job postings or of observations about them. The carve-outs in
section 3 take precedence over this section.

What is NOT claimed
-------------------
The individual job postings are facts published by third-party employers. No
ownership is claimed over any individual posting, over the fact that a given
employer is hiring, or over any employer's trademarks or names. Anyone is free
to go to the same ~90 sources and collect the same postings themselves.

What IS claimed
---------------
Two things, both of them original work rather than found facts:

  (a) The compilation — the selection, aggregation, normalisation, de-duplication
      and arrangement of postings drawn from ~90 separate sources into a single
      structured set.

  (b) The observational record — data that exists only because this project has
      been watching those sources daily and that cannot be reconstructed after
      the fact by scraping them today: first-observed and last-observed dates,
      posting lifetimes and closure dates, appearance/disappearance histories,
      staleness scores, hiring-velocity figures, and machine classifications of
      postings.

Permitted without asking
------------------------
  - Reading and browsing the site.
  - Personal, non-commercial use, including saving postings you are interested
    in and applying to them.
  - Quoting figures or excerpts in research, journalism, teaching or reporting,
    with attribution to JobFinder and the date of collection.
  - Reproducing the code under section 1 and running it against the original
    sources to build your own dataset.

Not permitted without prior written permission
----------------------------------------------
  - Bulk or systematic extraction of the data, whether in one request or many.
  - Redistributing, republishing or mirroring these data files, in whole or in
    substantial part, including in a modified or reformatted form.
  - Building a derived dataset or a competing product or service from them.
  - Using them to train or fine-tune machine-learning models.
  - Removing or obscuring attribution.

Permission may be requested through this repository's issue tracker.

Change of terms as of 2026-07-24
--------------------------------
Until 2026-07-24 the README of this repository stated that the data was
available under the Open Database License v1.0 (ODbL). That statement is
withdrawn and replaced by this section for all data published on or after
2026-07-24. Copies obtained before that date were received under ODbL and keep
those terms; ODbL is perpetual and irrevocable and nothing here purports to
revoke it retroactively. Data published from 2026-07-24 onward is governed by
this section only.


================================================================================
3. THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL — carve-outs
================================================================================

Section 2 does not apply to material this project did not produce:

  - data/avodata_ref.json — occupational reference data derived from
    avodata.labor.gov.il (Israeli Ministry of Labour processing of Central
    Bureau of Statistics 2023 data). Its embedded skills glossary comes from
    O*NET OnLine (USDOL/ETA, CC BY 4.0) and from the European Commission's ESCO
    classification, as republished by avodata. Redistributed under the terms of
    those sources, with their attribution preserved in the file itself. This
    file is deliberately public and is fetched by an external service; it must
    stay public.

  - Any third-party library, font or asset vendored into this repository keeps
    the licence it came with.

Employer names, logos and trademarks appearing in the data belong to their
respective owners and are used to identify the source of a posting.
